1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:03,930
A scientific workflow depends on much

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:05,290
more than its own code.

3
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:07,930
It needs container images, plugins,

4
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:10,090
reference files and input data all

5
00:00:10,110 --> 00:00:11,530
of them, at the moment it

6
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:12,170
runs.

7
00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:14,250
In a controlled environment with no

8
00:00:14,270 --> 00:00:16,650
internet, one missing piece stops the

9
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:17,610
analysis.

10
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:20,410
Nextflow Offline removes that risk:

11
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:22,730
everything is prepared first, then run

12
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:25,530
with confidence.

13
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:26,730
We'll follow one

14
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:28,010
real workflow:

15
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:30,650
a germline variant-calling test from nf-core

16
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:32,970
Sarek, using a small public test

17
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:33,370
dataset.

18
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:36,970
One release, from preparation to proven

19
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:37,770
result.

20
00:00:37,790 --> 00:00:39,290
Here is the whole picture,

21
00:00:39,310 --> 00:00:40,730
as three islands.

22
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:43,210
The connected mainland, with GitLab.

23
00:00:43,230 --> 00:00:44,090
The vault:

24
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:46,090
private AWS storage.

25
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:47,610
And the sealed island, where the

26
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:49,450
run happens, with no bridge to

27
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:50,730
the internet.

28
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:53,370
Everything starts in GitLab, the single

29
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,190
trusted home for the solution.

30
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:57,240
The workflow code lives in a

31
00:00:57,260 --> 00:00:59,080
GitLab repository.

32
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,080
Its container images live in the

33
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,160
GitLab container registry.

34
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:05,480
The reference data and test datasets

35
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:06,840
are hosted there too.

36
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:08,920
Content is scanned for security as

37
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,320
it arrives, and the operations team

38
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,480
reviews every finding.

39
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:15,480
From GitLab, a GitLab

40
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:17,000
Runner prepares the release.

41
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:19,720
The workflow code goes to AWS

42
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,360
CodeCommit.

43
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:22,760
Each container image goes to private

44
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:26,520
Amazon ECR, locked to its exact

45
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:26,760
identity.

46
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:29,640
The workflow, plugins, references and data

47
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:31,880
are packed into one bundle in

48
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,720
Amazon S3, with a fingerprint for

49
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:34,680
every file.

50
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,640
Together, they form one complete, self-contained

51
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:38,840
release.

52
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:40,280
Nothing is trusted

53
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,040
just because it was uploaded.

54
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:43,960
Every file and every image is

55
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:45,960
read back and compared against its

56
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:46,920
fingerprint.

57
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,760
Only when everything matches

58
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:50,840
is the release registered and ready

59
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:51,540
to run.

60
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:53,910
If anything is missing or different,

61
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:56,070
the release stops here, long before

62
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:57,750
it reaches a run.

63
00:01:57,770 --> 00:01:58,950
An approved run

64
00:01:58,970 --> 00:02:01,430
request in GitLab starts a controlled

65
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:03,670
run with its own unique identity.

66
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:06,310
AWS CodePipeline leads the run through

67
00:02:06,330 --> 00:02:07,490
ten gated stages.

68
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:09,990
It checks the source, the release,

69
00:02:10,010 --> 00:02:12,390
the data, the images and the

70
00:02:12,410 --> 00:02:15,670
runtime before anything executes, then validates

71
00:02:15,690 --> 00:02:17,990
the results and publishes the report.

72
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:19,510
CodeBuild does the work of each

73
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:23,030
stage, and AWS Systems Manager sends

74
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:25,110
commands to a private EC2

75
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:26,450
host with no internet

76
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:27,190
route.

77
00:02:27,210 --> 00:02:29,510
Private endpoints connect it only to

78
00:02:29,530 --> 00:02:32,470
the AWS services it needs.

79
00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:34,230
On the host, the bundle is

80
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:36,630
checked again against its fingerprints.

81
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:38,710
The exact images are loaded into

82
00:02:38,730 --> 00:02:40,870
an isolated container store.

83
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:43,910
Nextflow then runs in offline mode,

84
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:45,970
using only what was prepared.

85
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:47,510
Plugin downloads are switched

86
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:49,590
off, task containers have no network,

87
00:02:49,610 --> 00:02:51,250
and no image can be pulled.

88
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,980
Everything the analysis needs is already

89
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:54,780
there.

90
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:56,460
When the run ends, the result

91
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,300
is checked, not assumed.

92
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,100
The pipeline confirms the run succeeded,

93
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:02,940
that the offline controls were in

94
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:05,260
place, and that the expected outputs

95
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:05,900
exist.

96
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:07,660
The answer is a clear PASS

97
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,740
or FAIL, with an evidence report

98
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:11,180
the team can review.

99
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:12,780
And if anything tries to reach

100
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:14,300
the internet during a run?

101
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,900
There is nowhere to go.

102
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,140
The run stops, the failed stage

103
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:19,740
is named, and the result is

104
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:20,460
FAIL.

105
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:23,020
Never a silent half success.

106
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:24,860
So the journey is complete:

107
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:27,580
hosted and scanned in GitLab, prepared

108
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,700
and verified in AWS, executed offline

109
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:32,460
and backed by evidence.

110
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:34,780
Tested end to end, and repeatable

111
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:36,300
for every release.

112
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:37,500
Prepare once.

113
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:38,780
Verify everything.

114
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:45,800
Run with confidence.
